+## Kato applicability and the Version 11 consumer
+
+The active Kato dependency for this contribution is the [Version 11 repair](../../../manuscripts/quartic_v11.tex), not the smaller index family in the preserved [historical Version 10 pin](sources/quartic_v10.tex). The [application and consumer record](../../../docs/quartic-318-326/v11-consumer-binding/APPLICATION.json) binds the exact construction, norm identities, limiting class and downstream use. This adopts that specific repaired interface; it does not assert a complete revalidation of the restricted converse or a completed Lean witness-existence proof.
+
+The primary source is K. Kato, *Euler systems, Iwasawa theory, and Selmer groups*, Kodai Mathematical Journal 22 (1999), 313–372, [published PDF](https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/kodaimath1978/22/3/22_3_313/_pdf), DOI [10.2996/kmj/1138044090](https://doi.org/10.2996/kmj/1138044090). Its pinned PDF SHA-256 is `483dc18c56b2fe9cfac4e37f2e6594399625c3f0ea98a05bb69116377c67fc06`. Kato 2004, *p-adic Hodge theory and values of zeta functions of modular forms*, Astérisque 295, supplies the original class construction and Proposition 8.12 norm relation; the pinned PDF SHA-256 is `3c6e14b11fa60262db8aff782ce3cf4d83e9100c0be83621a7e4ce502cec605d`.
+
+The representation comes from E_l: y²=x(x−l)(x+3l), for each fixed positive squarefree l≡11 mod 12, not the different CM curve used to encode integer labels. Its j-invariant is 35152/9, hence nonintegral and non-CM. The retained representation argument uses Serre's open-image input to supply an open SL₂ subgroup in the image of the abelian-kernel Galois group. A quadratic character is trivial on that group, so the same subgroup survives twisting and contains a nonidentity unipotent with one-dimensional fixed space. Complex conjugation has one-dimensional eigenspaces. Kato 1999 Theorem 0.8 is used only at height-one localizations away from 2; no residual-irreducibility or integral-cokernel conclusion at primes containing 2 is inferred.
+
+Write D=2^a d for the primitive quadratic conductor, N for the conductor of this fixed elliptic curve, and retain the same symbol combination and smoothers c,d_s from K4–K5. Version 11 defines K8 for every positive m coprime to c*d_s. Its Kato 1999 subfamily uses N_K=2Nd and N'_K=c*d_s, so it includes every required m with N_K dividing m and gcd(m,N'_K)=1. The varying construction support prime(2NDm) is distinguished from the fixed inverted support N_K. Smoother primes are excluded from the index set, not silently added to the inverted support.
+
+For every prime ell not dividing N'_K, the two required norm cases are explicit. If ell divides m, the construction supports agree, including repeated bad, twisting and dyadic primes, and the norm factor is one. Otherwise N_K dividing m implies ell does not divide 2Nd, and Proposition 8.12 gives exactly P_ell(ell^−1 sigma_ell^−1), with P_ell(X)=1−a_ell chi(ell)X+ell X². Arithmetic Frobenius in P_ell and inverse cyclotomic deck action in the substitution are distinct conventions; the quadratic-twist factor is retained.
+
+We take the divisor parameter d=1 from Kato 1999 §0.4 in its §0.7 norm construction; this is different from the odd conductor part above. Equal supports at the DN_K 2^n and D2^n levels, followed by corestriction transitivity, give cor(y_(N_K 2^n))=y_(2^n) for n≥1. Thus its limiting class xi_K is the full-cohomology image of the actual retained dyadic class after the fixed lattice map, not an arbitrary proportional generator. There is no new Euler factor or field-degree multiplier. If the fixed lattice map 2^c_E is needed, the same map is used at every level; it is a unit at the away-from-two localizations used by K15, so the localized coordinate ideal remains the actual J.
+
+The theorem controls H²₀, the kernel of local restriction, and this actual retained-class coordinate ideal. It does not directly control all of H². The existing comparison of j_* and full cohomology, odd-place cone lengths and unused good-prime/fixed old-place corrections remains a separate retained argument. The same is true of retained-class nonvanishing and the representation hypotheses: the index/class repair does not independently re-prove them.
+
+The support-uniform integral assertion 2^c_E u^ev∈Z₂[[t]][G] uses the separate positive-complex construction and central calibration. Kato's away-from-two theorem does not prove that integral assertion or the full restricted converse. Compatible finite free models and determinant bases, uniformity in G and added conductor support, completed-localization comparison, the calibrated determinant and subsequent coefficient/limit arguments remain consequential dependencies. Constants may depend on the fixed E_l; no uniformity over all l is inferred.
+
+The exact restricted-converse, simultaneous-descent and pole/witness consumers are indexed in the application record. The concrete remaining completeness target is `∀ z : ℤ, CircuitReplay.HasSolution (Raw318FinalReplay.stage0 (Current318FinalPolynomial.scale : ℚ)) (z : ℚ)`: one compatible assignment satisfying all 288 actual source rows. The finite normalized-conic equivalence and canonical 326 lift are already checked; the latter preserves the input and adds no arithmetic premise. What remains is supplying compatible arithmetic pole inputs and the simultaneous point, prime-ray, guard, unit and anchor choices, then assembling every row. This interface adoption does not discharge that construction or independently validate all retained arithmetic inputs. The stored 318/326 polynomial definitions, coefficient tables and finite equivalence proofs are unchanged.
